---
import DocsLayout from "../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import Section from "../components/Section.astro";
import Code from "../components/Code.astro";
import TokenTable from "../components/api/TokenTable.astro";
import OptionsTable from "../components/api/OptionsTable.astro";
import { defineSections } from "../lib/sections";
import { route } from "../lib/catalogue";

const S = defineSections([
  { id: "anatomy", label: "Anatomy", heading: false },
  { id: "keyboard", label: "Keyboard" },
  { id: "tokens", label: "Tokens" },
  { id: "options", label: "Options" },
  { id: "methods", label: "Methods" },
]);
---
<DocsLayout page="menubar" sections={S}>
  <Section {...S.anatomy}>
        <p class="flow-text">
          A <code>.menubar</code> is an application's row of menus, such as
          File, Edit and View.
        </p>
        <p>
          Fill the bar with <code>.menu-trigger</code> buttons and name it with
          <code>aria-label</code>. Each button opens an ordinary
          <a href={route("menu")}>menu</a> written after the bar. The
          <code>Menubar</code> component adds the menubar and menuitem roles
          and the keyboard support below. <code>AutoInit()</code> starts it,
          or you can call <code>Expressive.Menubar.init(el)</code> yourself.
          For site navigation, use a
          <a href={route("mega_menu")}>mega menu</a> of links instead.
        </p>
        <div class="menubar" aria-label="Demo editor">
          <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="demo-file-menu">File</button>
          <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="demo-edit-menu">Edit</button>
          <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="demo-view-menu">View</button>
        </div>
        <menu id="demo-file-menu">
          <li><button type="button">New<kbd>Ctrl+N</kbd></button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Open<kbd>Ctrl+O</kbd></button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Save<kbd>Ctrl+S</kbd></button></li>
        </menu>
        <menu id="demo-edit-menu">
          <li><button type="button">Undo<kbd>Ctrl+Z</kbd></button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Redo<kbd>Ctrl+Y</kbd></button></li>
          <li class="divider" role="separator"></li>
          <li><button type="button">Cut<kbd>Ctrl+X</kbd></button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Copy<kbd>Ctrl+C</kbd></button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Paste<kbd>Ctrl+V</kbd></button></li>
        </menu>
        <menu id="demo-view-menu">
          <li><button type="button">Zoom in</button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Zoom out</button></li>
          <li><button type="button">Full screen</button></li>
        </menu>
<Code code={`
<div class="menubar" aria-label="Editor">
  <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="file-menu">File</button>
  <button type="button" class="menu-trigger" data-target="edit-menu">Edit</button>
</div>
<menu id="file-menu">
  <li><button type="button">New</button></li>
  <li><button type="button">Open</button></li>
</menu>
<menu id="edit-menu">
  <li><button type="button">Undo</button></li>
  <li><button type="button">Redo</button></li>
</menu>
`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.keyboard}>
        <ul>
          <li>The bar is one Tab stop, and it remembers the last item focused.</li>
          <li><kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> move along the bar and wrap. <kbd>Home</kbd> and <kbd>End</kbd> go to the ends. Both pairs mirror in right-to-left pages.</li>
          <li><kbd>Down</kbd>, <kbd>Enter</kbd> or <kbd>Space</kbd> opens a menu on its first item.</li>
          <li>In an open menu, <kbd>Left</kbd> and <kbd>Right</kbd> open the neighbouring menu, unless the row opens or closes a submenu.</li>
          <li><kbd>Escape</kbd> closes the menu and returns to its item.</li>
        </ul>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.tokens}>
        <TokenTable>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--md-comp-menubar-container-color</code></td>
            <td><code>--md-sys-color-surface-container</code></td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--md-comp-menubar-shape</code></td>
            <td>12px</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td><code>--md-comp-menubar-item-height</code></td>
            <td>40px</td>
          </tr>
        </TokenTable>
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.options}>
    <OptionsTable>
      <tr><td><code>menuOptions</code></td><td>Object</td><td><code>{"{ constrainWidth: false, coverTrigger: false }"}</code></td><td>Options passed to each child Menu. A supplied object replaces this default object; include both keys when retaining the content-width layout below the bar.</td></tr>
    </OptionsTable>
    <p>See <a href={route("menu")}>Menu options</a> for placement and callbacks.
      Child triggers take options from their Menubar, so their <code>data-menu-options</code> attributes do not apply.</p>
    <Code code={`const bar = Expressive.Menubar.init(document.querySelector('.menubar'), {
  menuOptions: { constrainWidth: false, coverTrigger: false, closeOnClick: false }
});`} />
  </Section>
  <Section {...S.methods}>
    <p><code>Expressive.Menubar.getInstance(el)</code> retrieves the instance.
      Call <code>bar.destroy()</code> when removing the bar. It destroys child Menus,
      removes managed tab stops and removes roles that initialization added.</p>
    <p>Open or close an individual menu through
      <code>Expressive.Menu.getInstance(trigger).open()</code> or <code>close()</code>.
      Menubar has no separate open or close methods. Its <code>el</code> and
      <code>options</code> properties follow the shared component contract.</p>
  </Section>
</DocsLayout>
